Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs YAML: values, free text and validation
	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-24
		Description: STXT and YAML compared with examples: implicit typing versus text values, block scalars versus literal blocks, attack surface, and validation inside the language.

	Header: @STXT@ vs YAML

	Assert >>
		YAML and @STXT@ use indentation to define the hierarchy.
		The mental model is different: YAML has **maps and lists**, like JSON, and @STXT@ has **trees of text nodes**, like XML.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		A service descriptor in YAML:

	Yaml >>
		service: billing
		replicas: 2
		ports:
		  - 8080
		  - 8443
		maintainer: ana@example.com
		notes: |
		  Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
		  The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Service: billing
			Replicas: 2
			Ports:
				Port: 8080
				Port: 8443
			Maintainer: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		A simple document looks alike in the two. The differences are these:

		|                 | YAML                                                                | @STXT@                                                                                    |
		| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
		| Values          | The spelling decides the type: `2` is an integer and `"2"` a string | Every value is text. The type, when needed, is declared by the template                   |
		| Lists           | Two syntaxes: block (`- 8080`) and flow (`[8080, 8443]`)            | There is no list syntax: a repeated child is the list ([FAQ](faq#why-are-there-no-lists)) |
		| Indentation     | Any number of spaces per level, and no tabs                         | One tab or four spaces per level                                                          |
		| Multi-line text | Block scalars, with several indicators                              | A block node `>>`                                                                         |
		| Namespaces      | None                                                                | Part of the language ([STXT-SPEC §7](stxt-core-ref#s7))                                   |
		| Schemas         | External tools                                                      | Part of the language, and optional                                                        |

	Subheader: When the spelling changes the meaning

	Content >>
		In YAML, an unquoted value is converted to a type according to its form.
		The rules changed between versions 1.1 and 1.2 of the language:

	Yaml >>
		country: NO         # false in YAML 1.1; the string "NO" in 1.2
		version: 3.10       # the number 3.1 in both
		window: 12:30       # the integer 750 in YAML 1.1 (sexagesimal); a string in 1.2
		enabled: yes        # true in 1.1; the string "yes" in 1.2

	Content >>
		Keeping `NO`, `3.10` or `12:30` as text requires quoting them.
		In @STXT@ a value is always the characters written:

	Code >>
		Settings:
			Country: NO
			Version: 3.10
			Window: 12:30
			Enabled: yes

	Content >>
		| Node name | Node value |
		| --------- | ---------- |
		| Country   | `NO`       |
		| Version   | `3.10`     |
		| Window    | `12:30`    |
		| Enabled   | `yes`      |

		If `Enabled` must be a boolean, the template says so (`Enabled: (1) BOOLEAN`).
		In that case the validator rejects `yes`, and does not convert it.

	Subheader: Multi-line text

	Content >>
		A YAML block scalar combines several indicators: the style (`|` literal or `>` folded),
		the final line break (`-`, `+` or none) and, when needed, the indentation:

	Yaml >>
		literal: |
		  first line
		  second line
		folded: >
		  first line
		  second line
		kept: |+
		  first line

		next: value

	Content >>
		Each combination produces a different string:

		| Key       | Value                                                     |
		| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
		| `literal` | `"first line\nsecond line\n"`                             |
		| `folded`  | `"first line second line\n"`                              |
		| `kept`    | `"first line\n\n"` (it keeps the empty line that follows) |

		@STXT@ has a single form, the block node. The text is literal, with no variants and no escape characters:

	Code >>
		Notes >>
			first line
			second line

	Content >>
		Additional indentation and intermediate empty lines are kept. Trailing whitespace
		on each line and trailing empty lines are discarded ([STXT-SPEC §10](stxt-core-ref#s10)).

	Subheader: Security

	Content >>
		YAML includes two features that @STXT@ does not have:

		* **Anchors and aliases** (`&a`, `*a`): a small document can expand into a huge structure in memory.
		* **Type tags** (`!!python/object`, `!ruby/object`): implementation-specific tags have allowed
		  arbitrary objects to be instantiated while loading a document. Hence the `safe_load` functions of the libraries.

		@STXT@ has no references, anchors, tags, inclusion or entities. Parsing is linear, line by line
		and in a single pass ([STXT-SPEC §15](stxt-core-ref#s15)).

	Subheader: Validation

	Content >>
		YAML is validated with external tools, usually JSON Schema over the loaded structure.
		In @STXT@ schemas are part of the language. The descriptor from the beginning, with a namespace:

	Code >>
		Service (com.example.deploy): billing
			Replicas: 2
			Port: 8080
			Port: 8443
			Maintainer: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.deploy
			Structure >>
				Service (com.example.deploy):
					Replicas: (1) NATURAL
					Port: (+) NATURAL
					Maintainer: (1) EMAIL
					Notes: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		The `Ports` container is not needed here: the `(+)` cardinality already says that `Port` repeats.
		A document with errors does not validate:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: this document does not validate
		Service (com.example.deploy): billing
			# `two` is not a NATURAL
			Replicas: two
			# At least one `Port` is missing
			# `ana` is not an EMAIL
			Maintainer: ana
			# `Region` is not in the template
			Region: eu-west-1

	Subheader: What about TOML?

	Content >>
		TOML shares its goal with @STXT@: files that a person writes and reads.
		The hierarchy is not in the indentation but in headers that repeat the full path of their table:

	Toml >>
		[[job]]
		name = "database"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "pg_dump acme > acme.sql"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "gzip acme.sql"

		[[job]]
		name = "documents"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs"

	Content >>
		Each `[[job.step]]` belongs to the last `[[job]]` opened. In @STXT@ each level is written once,
		and a child belongs to the node it is indented under:

	Code >>
		Backup:
			Job: database
				Step: pg_dump acme > acme.sql
				Step: gzip acme.sql
			Job: documents
				Step: tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs

	Content >>
		As in YAML, the type of a value is decided by its spelling: `14` is an integer and `"14"` a string.
		Strings always go between quotes, and multi-line ones cannot be indented with their table.
		It has no namespaces or schemas.

	Subheader: What about NestedText?

	Content >>
		NestedText makes several of the decisions of @STXT@: every value is text, with no implicit types
		or escape characters, and the structure is in the indentation. The descriptor from the beginning:

	Listing >>
		service: billing
		replicas: 2
		ports:
		    - 8080
		    - 8443
		maintainer: ana@example.com
		notes:
		    > Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
		    > The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Service: billing
			Replicas: 2
			Ports:
				Port: 8080
				Port: 8443
			Maintainer: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		`2` and `8080` are strings in both, and multi-line text is literal: in NestedText
		each line starts with `>`, and in @STXT@ it goes under a block node.

		NestedText keeps the YAML data model (dictionaries, lists and strings), and leaves types
		and validation to the application. It has no namespaces or schemas.
